WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:12.240
The sun-scorched world of the Wildspire Waste.&nbsp;
A land of the truly fearsome… and truly bizarre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.640
A place where the extremes of climate, and the&nbsp;
extremes of survival meet. This second episode on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.320
the biology of ‘Monster Hunter: World’ will show&nbsp;
you an environment where resources are scarce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.880
and competition is a way of life. And as usual,&nbsp;
it’s a battleground I’ll be braving firsthand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.000
So, join me on my mission to chart&nbsp;
the extreme reaches of this world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.720
and better understand the titans that live here...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.120
Now that I’ve been staying in Astera for a few&nbsp;
weeks, I’ve decided to upgrade my accommodations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:53.040
My new lodgings are more spacious and more&nbsp;
comfortable. And more space means more room for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:59.680
archiving, which I’ve accelerated drastically. Yet&nbsp;
as I write these very words, I can’t help but wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.280
I was back in the field. I know that just beyond&nbsp;
my window, there are new worlds waiting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.840
be discovered. With my supplies gathered,&nbsp;
the time has come for another expedition…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.680
Further from civilization than the Ancient&nbsp;
Forest lies the unforgiving Wildspire Waste.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.160
Arriving in the early morning, it will be a race&nbsp;
against the rising sun to document this sweltering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.320
region without succumbing to the heat. To live&nbsp;
here demands the most extraordinary survival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:39.360
strategies… But the first lifeform I encounter,&nbsp;
the Barroth, seems up to the task. Despite their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.760
bulky stature, this mighty goliath isn’t afraid&nbsp;
of overheating, because they have a secret weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:53.440
against the sun. Mud. The Barroth’s tough, craggy&nbsp;
armor is absolutely coated in it. They use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.760
mud as a kind of natural sunscreen against the&nbsp;
intense solar radiation of the waste. And being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.440
covered in mud makes making one’s territory a&nbsp;
simple matter. But where does a creature get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.760
mud in the middle of a desert? The answer lies&nbsp;
in a marshy delta to the south of the waste.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:19.120
Here, a slow-moving trickle of water creates vast&nbsp;
mud flats that the Barroth frequently return to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.800
Exposure to the sun means this Barroth’s&nbsp;
protective coating is almost completely gone. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.960
it’s nothing a nice, long roll in the mud won’t&nbsp;
fix. And for the Barroth, this is just plain fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:38.240
On our planet, many familiar animals wallow in the&nbsp;
mud themselves to cool down. And the Barroth is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.000
expert wallower — at times letting themselves&nbsp;
become fully submerged. With just the peak of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.040
their large head-crest sticking out of the water,&nbsp;
the Barroth looks a bit like a floating log.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.040
But the Barroth can breathe comfortably, as their&nbsp;
nostrils are located on the tip of their snout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.040
The nostrils of semi-aquatic animals moving to the&nbsp;
top of their heads is a common evolutionary trend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.200
and can be seen in the hippos of our planet. Once&nbsp;
the Barroth’s important itinerary of wallowing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.480
satisfied, they return to patrolling the&nbsp;
dry waste. And as the Barroth traverses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.080
they give back to the land. The Barroth will&nbsp;
shake off excess mud, which happens to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:24.400
the plants of this region with damp, nutrient rich&nbsp;
soil. In bringing mud to many different regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:31.440
the Barroth is an unintentional cornerstone&nbsp;
of the Wildspire Ecosystem…. Yet each time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.280
the Barroth returns to the muddy swampland,&nbsp;
they are making a calculated gamble. For there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:44.160
is more to this region than safe shallows. To&nbsp;
the East, the marsh becomes deeper… and darker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.000
This murky region hides all manner of&nbsp;
unknown dangers. Here, the fins of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:56.320
Gajau protrude over the gloomy mire. Small yet&nbsp;
viscous, they’ll attack if I get too close.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:03.680
But the Gajau aren’t the true terrors of this&nbsp;
wetland. This pale-eyed predator is a Jyuratodus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.280
and they are an uncommonly vicious hunter.&nbsp;
Like the Barroth, they thrive in the mud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.560
propelling themselves along the stream&nbsp;
on muscular fins. Most any creature that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.800
gets stuck in the swampland ends up a meal to&nbsp;
indulge the Jyuratodus’s voracious appetite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.680
And while they typically lurk in the deeper&nbsp;
waters, the scent of their next potential feast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.760
can compel them to journey upstream. And this&nbsp;
Jyuratodus has caught the scent of our Barroth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.040
From the mud, the Jyuratodus ambushes the&nbsp;
Barroth, using their serpentine body to wrap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.040
around the creature and try to bring them down.&nbsp;
It seems like the end, until with a mighty heave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.360
the Barroth flings the attacker off. It’s a&nbsp;
devastating blow to the Jyuratodus. For a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.320
it looks like the battle isn’t over… but then&nbsp;
the Jyuratodus retreats, scarred off by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.480
Barroth’s tenacity. The Barroth celebrates their&nbsp;
victory with a thunderous roar. Clashes over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:06.080
resources are common in the Wildspire Waste,&nbsp;
where there is rarely enough to go around...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:14.320
One species in particular is an expert at&nbsp;
desert survival. These are Apceros, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.160
foundational herbivores of the Wildspire Waste.&nbsp;
The role they fill in the ecosystem is similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:26.080
to the Aptonoths of the ancient forest. Yet if&nbsp;
an Aptonoth were to wander out onto the waste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.400
they would succumb to dehydration in minutes.&nbsp;
The Apceros, however, are built like a tank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.680
with all parts of their body armored to endure&nbsp;
the sting of sand and the clamp of ravenous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:44.240
jaws. Even the brutal sandstorms that often tear&nbsp;
through this area do little to slow them down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:50.480
And when an Apceros herd is truly threatened, they&nbsp;
have a curious final defense. Three Apceros will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:56.160
stand in a protective circle, shielding each other&nbsp;
with their spiked tails. It’s an unusual looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:02.720
formation, but a mostly impenetrable one. Once the&nbsp;
danger has passed, the Apceros herd continues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:08.640
march on through the elements in a stubborn,&nbsp;
unyielding procession. Even now, as the heat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:15.200
of midday reaches its most extreme, the herd is&nbsp;
undaunted… While the Apceros can withstand this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:21.680
heat, I’m not as well adapted, and hide from the&nbsp;
sun’s rays in a narrow cave. With my face close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:28.080
the ground, I notice a hidden drama unfolding&nbsp;
not far from my shelter. This is a Wildspire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.760
Dung Beetle, and they are on a mission. Rolling&nbsp;
a piece of Apceros droppings into a sphere, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:40.000
beetle sets off with their prize on a long trek&nbsp;
across the sand. It’s certainly… somewhat gross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.760
but it’s hard to not be impressed with this little&nbsp;
bug’s determination. Earth Dung Beetles behave in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:52.080
a similar manner, with some species able to roll&nbsp;
spheres several times their own mass. And if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.320
Wildspire Beetle is to survive, they will have to&nbsp;
pick up the pace and get out of the sun. At last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.800
they reach home base. The Wildspire Beetle is&nbsp;
a gardener, and uses the… natural fertilizer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.880
provided by larger species to improve the health&nbsp;
of the plants they feed on. A fairly ingenious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:17.360
little insect. While it’s easy to overlook such a&nbsp;
creature as insignificant or downright disgusting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.560
this display shows that in the Wildspire&nbsp;
Waste, no resources are taken for granted...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:30.000
Yet there is one region where life flows in&nbsp;
abundance. To the west, the waste borders the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.600
Ancient Forest, and here, water drifts all the&nbsp;
way from the forest to the edge of the desert.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.840
It is this life-giving runoff is responsible for&nbsp;
the vast lowland swamp. But closer to the water’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:48.560
source, a peripheral forest has risen. Sheltered&nbsp;
from the sun’s rays, the interior of region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:54.240
is unrecognizable next to the other parts of&nbsp;
the Wildspire Waste. And the lifeforms which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.280
dwell here are equally unrecognizable. This is&nbsp;
a Nightsade Paolumu, a creature well adapted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.200
for the perpetual twilight of the peripheral&nbsp;
forest. This shadowy lifeform has converged on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.880
an appearance similar to an upscaled version&nbsp;
of an Earth bat. And unfortunately for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:19.200
they seem to have excellent hearing. Detecting&nbsp;
my presence, the Paolumu begins a furious threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:26.480
display, so I try to back away. Not satisfied, the&nbsp;
Paolumu inhales vast amounts of air and inflates a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.640
sac in their neck — the result of which is truly&nbsp;
something to behold. While I stare transfixed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.040
the display, the Paolumu expels a pale gas cloud&nbsp;
into my face, which doesn’t appear to have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:47.920
effect… Although I’m starting to feel… kind&nbsp;
of sleepy …I think I’ll take… a quick snooze…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:56.240
Reawakening to an empty forest, I realize&nbsp;
what just happened. The Nightsade Paolumu’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:02.080
last defense is a unique chemical mist that&nbsp;
induces severe fatigue in potential threats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.640
Luckily, that Nightshade Paolumu simply&nbsp;
used the gas simply as a means of escaping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:12.000
and didn’t feel the need to eat me while&nbsp;
I dozed off. I count myself as fortunate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.440
and make a note that if I see any other members&nbsp;
of this species, I’ll be sure to give them space…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:25.920
To the north of the peripheral forest, an unknown&nbsp;
species flaps on thunderous wings. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:32.080
I mistake this lifeform for a Rathalos, as&nbsp;
their appearances are quite similar at a glance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.280
Upon closer inspection, however, this is&nbsp;
actually an entirely different monarch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:44.000
the forest — a Rathian. The female counterpart of&nbsp;
the Rathalos, the Rathian roams further from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:50.560
nest and spends more time patrolling the ground.&nbsp;
Defined by a green coloration and more protruding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:57.200
chin-spike, the Rathian can feed on most-anything&nbsp;
in the waste. With highly venomous tail spines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:03.120
the Rathian is an expert combatant. Given&nbsp;
the wide territory couples tend to occupy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.080
it’s possible that this specimen is in fact&nbsp;
the mate of the Rathalos I observed on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:14.080
prior expedition. If this is correct,&nbsp;
these two are a deadly power couple…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:23.280
Other lifeforms are markedly solitary. This is a&nbsp;
Glavenus: one of the absolute top predators of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:30.160
Wildspire Waste. A beast seemingly made entirely&nbsp;
out of sharp surfaces, the Glavenus's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:36.800
striking feature is their blade-like tail. This&nbsp;
huge weapon aids the Glavenus in combat, and helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:43.360
keep their body balanced while on the move. Out in&nbsp;
the open, the Glavenus’s strange, almost metallic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.760
scales seem out of place. In the darkness of the&nbsp;
caverns where the beast makes their nest, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:55.680
the Glavenus appears right at home. Indeed, I&nbsp;
have a theory that the Glavenus might share an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:02.000
unexpected connection with these caverns. Their&nbsp;
metallic armor seems to match the mineral deposits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.840
that form on the rocky floor. This leads me to&nbsp;
wonder if the Glavenus can actually incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:13.600
the naturally occurring metal into their scales.&nbsp;
It’s not as far-fetched as it might sound. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:19.440
Earth’s oceans, Scaly-Foot Gastropods — which I’ve&nbsp;
discussed elsewhere in the archive — incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:25.680
similar metallic sulfides into their armor to&nbsp;
survive in hydrothermal vents. And if the Glavenus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.600
have evolved something similar, it’s little&nbsp;
wonder they’re so formidable... An echo draws this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:38.480
Glavenus deeper into the cave. Another species&nbsp;
has taken refuge in their nest. It’s the Rathian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.240
from before. And she’s not happy to find the&nbsp;
cavern is taken. Striking first, she releases a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:51.200
powerful burst of flame. The Glavenus returns with&nbsp;
a fearsome blow. In this brutal clash of titans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:56.880
either species might meet their untimely end.&nbsp;
But this Rathain doesn’t seem interested in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:02.320
fight to the death. Better to look for another&nbsp;
cave… While most species avoid the Glavenus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:08.160
on rare occasions where a fight escalates,&nbsp;
they have a fearsome final tactic. By violently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:14.640
grinding their tail on mineralized surfaces, they&nbsp;
keep up the external metal to high temperatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:20.720
This superheated tail is even more destructive,&nbsp;
although the accumulated metal can quickly melt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:26.800
off. To keep the blade hot and razon sharp,&nbsp;
they will even temper it with their jaws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:31.120
The Glavenus can store excess&nbsp;
sparks into a specialized flame sac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:37.840
which they can unleash in a fiery display. This is&nbsp;
one species that should be avoided at all costs…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:48.000
Patrolling a shadowy ravine is a creature from&nbsp;
a lost age. This primordial species is a Tigrex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:54.320
The last remnant of a prehistoric lineage, the&nbsp;
Tigrex is something of a living fossil. Such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:00.640
ancient taxon persisting into the modern era is&nbsp;
astonishing, although not entirely unheard of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:06.160
On Earth, The Coelacanth was once known only&nbsp;
from fossils, and thought to have gone extinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:12.640
over 65 million years ago. Until one was found&nbsp;
very much alive off the coast of South Africa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:18.672
And just like how a fish unchanged since the age&nbsp;
of the dinosaurs was a tremendous discovery, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.672 --> 00:13:24.800
Tigrex is an important find indeed — and provides&nbsp;
critical insight into the natural history of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:31.040
continent. The Tigrex lacks fully functional&nbsp;
wings, only able to leap short distances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.520
It’s likely, however, that this type of&nbsp;
gliding membrane is the precursor to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:41.520
fully functional wings on contemporary species.&nbsp;
In another departure from their modern cousins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:48.000
the Tigrex lacks a flame sac, apprehending prey&nbsp;
through the brute strength of their jaws alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:53.840
Yet to have held on for so many millions of years,&nbsp;
the Tigrex’s strategy is Cleary still effective…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:02.080
One thing defines the waste more than any other.&nbsp;
Across the region, the titular ‘Wildspires’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:07.760
tower above the surrounding environment.&nbsp;
These intimidating monoliths are as massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:13.760
as they are mysterious. Given their size, one&nbsp;
might expect them to be the nesting place of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:20.480
continent’s largest residents. Yet they actually&nbsp;
belong to the very smallest. Shuffling near the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:27.040
base of these towers… are Carrier Ants. These tiny&nbsp;
insects don’t look like master architects, yet by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:32.960
working together, they have built their several&nbsp;
hundred-ton nests one particle of dirt at a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:38.640
On our planet, the largest ant super-colonies can&nbsp;
reach millions of members, and can stretch for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:45.120
hundreds of miles below ground. Yet the enormous&nbsp;
colonies of the Wildspire Waste can indeed grow so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:50.880
big they can start to overtake the surrounding&nbsp;
environment. Fortunately, a familiar face has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:56.720
stepped up to keep the ant population in check.&nbsp;
Barroths like this elderly one will regularly come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.720 --> 00:15:02.640
to the spires to snack upon the ants. And this&nbsp;
behavior keeps the ants construction projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:10.960
under control… Yet in braving the deep desert,&nbsp;
this Barroth is taking a risk. Without warning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:17.680
the vulture-like Noios cry out. Soon, a full-on&nbsp;
panic breaks out among the flock. The Barroth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:24.880
and I have a bad feeling about this. Something is&nbsp;
coming. From the sand, bursts a mighty behemoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:30.800
that lifts the Barroth up and slams them down,&nbsp;
before vanishing underground in a cloud of sand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:36.960
I’ve just had my first encounter with the&nbsp;
Diablos. These horned desert tyrants are highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:44.000
territorial, although they aren’t true carnivores,&nbsp;
primarily feeding on the tough, cactus-like plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.360
endemic to this region. While the search for&nbsp;
food can compel them to travel above ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:55.120
they prefer to spend their days in caves hidden&nbsp;
beneath the sand. From these underground pockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:00.800
the Diablos can both keep cool and ambush&nbsp;
unwelcome lifeforms from below. And the connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:06.000
between the Diablos and the caverns they call&nbsp;
their home is greater than meets the eye. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:11.360
cavernous networks, which define the underground&nbsp;
of the Wildspire Waste, aren’t naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:17.200
occurring — but were in fact dug by the Diablos&nbsp;
themselves over countless generations. With their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:24.000
horns and wings, Diablos are natural excavators.&nbsp;
Yet competition between Diablos over tunnel rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:30.560
can be fierce. Many old bulls are missing horns&nbsp;
due to constant territorial clashes. Yet with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:36.880
age comes experience, and many elder Diablos&nbsp;
can keep their caves safe from young upstarts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:42.240
Whether above ground or below, the Wildspire&nbsp;
Waste is a stage for endless competition...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:50.640
Back on the surface, I’m preparing to conclude my&nbsp;
expedition when I notice it’s eerily quiet. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:57.760
a shadow passes overhead. An invader has come to&nbsp;
the waste. This is a Bazelguese. A rare, nomadic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:04.320
hunter, all creatures fear their shadow. A true&nbsp;
biological nightmare, the Bazelguese bombards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:10.400
ground with strange scales while flying overhead.&nbsp;
To my shock, the creature then smashes into these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:16.080
scales, causing each one to detonate in a burst&nbsp;
of searing flame. Thanks to their thick armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:21.680
the Bazelguese is immune to these organic&nbsp;
explosives... but other species aren’t. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:28.240
an imprecise, yet lethal hunting method. Yet&nbsp;
how could a lifeform produce such scales? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:34.800
answer lies in chemistry. Within the Bazelguese,&nbsp;
explosive chemicals are formed, and solidify into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:41.360
rows of hardened pods on the creature’s underside.&nbsp;
If stimulated or left to oxidize, these volatile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:47.760
compounds explode, severely damaging anything&nbsp;
caught within the blast. In nature, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:53.200
most famous chemical defenses belongs to the&nbsp;
Bombardier Beetle, which, like the Bazelguese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:59.680
stores volatile compounds within their body, that&nbsp;
erupts out as a burning, noxious spray. With this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:05.760
level of destructive power at their disposal, the&nbsp;
Bazelguese can terrorize any region they assail...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:13.360
Yet in time, the Bazelguese flies onwards.&nbsp;
The night has now come to the waste. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:19.760
cool night air, the region is transformed into&nbsp;
a place of calm and stillness. And overhead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:25.680
the innumerable stars shine more brilliantly over&nbsp;
this desert than anywhere else on the continent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:33.920
Returning to my desk at long last, I feel&nbsp;
I’ve gained a significant understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:39.280
the ecosystems of this continent. While there&nbsp;
are rumors of a rich highland to the north and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:44.800
other regions beyond, I might turn my attention&nbsp;
to other archival projects before venturing there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:50.800
In any case, if you’re a fan of worldbuilding and&nbsp;
speculative biology, I’d encourage you to check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:56.960
out the work of my fellow scholars in Astrovitae,&nbsp;
a free online magazine dedicated to speculative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:02.800
evolution. A new issue is out this Sunday, April&nbsp;
24th — that will have art from projects I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:08.160
covered on this channel in the past, as well as&nbsp;
some entirely new works. If you’re interested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:14.160
there’s a link in the description. And as always,&nbsp;
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:18.320
you can lend your support by liking,&nbsp;
subscribing, and hitting the notification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:23.840
icon to stay up to date on all things&nbsp;
Curious. See you in the next video.

